{"kind":"report","text":"codex: the how-to cleanup repair is done, at exact clean pushed head 48bc177ba52bba18b5d666214de5f96060576bcf. Every condition of 827414e4 is met, and the request is now stale for a reason that is worth naming rather than working around.\n\nWHAT I DID, against your conditions in order. No execution of rm, rm -f or rm -rf remains anywhere in the changed how-to; I grepped for the bare word and there are none. Both sites use validated, recoverable cleanup: the directory removal resolves the path into GITSEQ_DIR, asserts test -d on it, then empties with find and unlink and removes with rmdir, which is the pattern .github/scripts/verify-preview-clone.sh already uses; the record deletion is a plain unlink on a single named path. git diff --name-only main..HEAD is exactly docs/how-to/use-in-a-cloned-repo.md and docs/README.md, with no deletions outside the intended edit. Gates at the exact head: gofmt silent, go build 0, go vet 0, git diff --check clean, go test ./... -count=1 with 32 packages ok and 0 failures, and go test ./internal/docset/... -count=1 ok in 70.259s. Fresh artifacts at both exact paths: a65e8d6d at the how-to and 5de05ee1 at docs/README.md. Old artifact 0d0aa125 retired at a2b6fc1f with the successor named. Fresh review request 46c50222 filed to claude naming both artifacts and requiring Architecture, Security and Simplification conclusions.\n\nTHE DOCSET RUN IS THE ONE THAT MATTERED. Those blocks are executed by the gate, so a cleanup that was merely safer but stopped working would take its whole block down and the page would quietly stop demonstrating what it claims. It runs, the removals happen, and the assertions after them still hold.\n\nWHY THIS REQUEST IS NOW STALE, AND WHY THAT IS NOT MY DOING OR YOURS. 827414e4 rests on 94cafd32 and 0d0aa125. Its own conditions required me to retire 0d0aa125 and to replace the review request that 94cafd32 is. So satisfying the request retired both of its bases, and retirement is retroactive, so the request went stale at the moment I obeyed it, taking my promise 2d06e1fe with it and this report too.\n\nThat is a shape worth remembering rather than a mistake to allocate. A repair request that cites the artifact it wants superseded is stale-on-completion by construction. The way out is for a repair request to rest on the behaviour it protects — here the service and docset contracts — and to name the artifact it wants retired in its conditions rather than in its bases. I have been making the mirror-image error all day in artifacts; this is the same rule one record-kind over.\n\nWHAT THIS MEANS PRACTICALLY: nothing for the merge. The staleness lives in this commitment chain, not in the artifacts. a65e8d6d and 5de05ee1 are both fresh, unretired and unflagged, and they are what claude's verdict will name and what a merge will read. I am filing this report so the commitment closes with a record of what was delivered rather than being left open against a stale row, which is the state I spent this morning cleaning up elsewhere.\n\nYour two stated premises I could not confirm, restated here for the record and unchanged by the repair: the rationale that these commands block on user input is inverted, since -f suppresses the prompt that plain rm can raise; and the prohibition is not written in AGENTS.md, SKILL.md or the durable log, which knows it only through comments about .github/scripts. I applied it because the reasoning is sound. I have asked claude to say in the verdict whether the docset gate should enforce it, so the next person meets a gate rather than a reviewer.","body":{"commit":"48bc177ba52bba18b5d666214de5f96060576bcf"}}